From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] soc: cix: only deny generic ACPI ids on real Sky1 firmware

device_deny_id_init() registered its deny handlers unconditionally, and
DENY_PCIE denies PNP0A03 -- the standard ACPI PCI root bridge. On Sky1 that is
deliberate, because CIXH2020 must own the controller instead. On any other ACPI
machine it means the PCI bus is never enumerated at all.

Measured under QEMU virt (MCFG OEM "BOCHS") with this kernel: the boot reached
"ACPI: MCFG table detected, 1 entries" and then produced no PCI root bridge,
/sys/bus/pci/devices was empty, and /proc/partitions listed nothing -- no xhci,
no virtio_blk, no installer medium. debian-installer correctly reported "No
device for installation media was detected", which read like an image bug and
was not one. Booting the same kernel with the existing pcie_pnp_en early_param
(which repoints DENY_PCIE at the CIX ids) brought up PCI Root Bridge [PCI0],
xhci_hcd, virtio_blk and the USB medium as /dev/sda.

Gate the whole deny-handler registration on the same MCFG OEM check that
pci-sky1-acpi.c already uses for its PNP0A08 block, so Sky1 behaviour is
unchanged and every other platform keeps working generic enumeration. This is
what makes the shipped kernel testable in KVM at all.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [oe-specific]
Downstream fix to a downstream patch (0066); the deny list does not exist
upstream.

Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
--- a/drivers/soc/cix/acpi/cix-acpi-plat.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/cix/acpi/cix-acpi-plat.c
@@ -110,10 +110,51 @@
 	}
 };
 
+/*
+ * True only on real Sky1 firmware.
+ *
+ * The deny handlers below exist to stop the GENERIC ACPI enumerators from
+ * claiming devices that the CIX vendor drivers must own.  That is correct on
+ * Sky1 and actively harmful anywhere else: DENY_PCIE blocks PNP0A03, the
+ * standard PCI root bridge, so on any non-Sky1 ACPI machine the PCI bus is
+ * never enumerated at all -- no host controllers, no storage, no network.
+ *
+ * Measured under QEMU virt (MCFG OEM "BOCHS"): with the deny active the kernel
+ * reported "ACPI: MCFG table detected" and then nothing, /sys/bus/pci/devices
+ * was empty and /proc/partitions listed no devices whatsoever.  With the deny
+ * skipped the same kernel brings up "ACPI: PCI Root Bridge [PCI0]", xhci_hcd,
+ * virtio_blk and the USB medium.
+ *
+ * Detection matches pci-sky1-acpi.c, which already guards its own PNP0A08
+ * block the same way.
+ */
+static bool sky1_firmware_detected(void)
+{
+	struct acpi_table_header *header;
+	bool detected;
+
+	if (acpi_disabled)
+		return false;
+
+	if (ACPI_FAILURE(acpi_get_table(ACPI_SIG_MCFG, 0, &header)))
+		return false;
+
+	detected = !memcmp(header->oem_id, "CIXTEK", 6) &&
+		   !memcmp(header->oem_table_id, "SKY1EDK2", 8);
+	acpi_put_table(header);
+
+	return detected;
+}
+
 static void device_deny_id_init(void)
 {
 	int i;
 
+	if (!sky1_firmware_detected()) {
+		pr_info("cix-acpi-plat: not Sky1 firmware, leaving generic ACPI enumeration alone\n");
+		return;
+	}
+
 	if (pcie_pnp_en)
 		acpi_deny_handler[DENY_PCIE].ids = pcie_cdns_deny_ids;
 
